error[NK1102]: `>` is given `i64?`, which may be `null`.
  --> tests/a_field_read_through_an_index_or_a_call_does_not_narrow.nika:8:33
   |
 8 |     if v[i].n != null && v[i].n > 0 {
   |                                 ^^^
   |
   = note: A `T?` is a type of its own (Part I 2.3) and has none of `T`'s operators. It is a `T` only where a test before the use proves it is not `null`, as `x != null && x > 3` does; a `match` arm's name binds the whole `T?`.
   = note: This read goes through an index, so it is not a path of fields from a name: an index is computed anew at each read, and a `null` test of it proves nothing here (ADR-382 D4). Copy it into a local and test that: `let x = …`.
   = help: Say what an absent value counts as first: `(x ?? 0) + 1`, or take the value out once with `let v = x ?? return`.
error[NK1102]: `>` is given `i64?`, which may be `null`.
   --> tests/a_field_read_through_an_index_or_a_call_does_not_narrow.nika:11:37
    |
 11 |     if make().n != null && make().n > 0 {
    |                                     ^^^
    |
    = note: A `T?` is a type of its own (Part I 2.3) and has none of `T`'s operators. It is a `T` only where a test before the use proves it is not `null`, as `x != null && x > 3` does; a `match` arm's name binds the whole `T?`.
    = note: This read goes through a call's result, so it is not a path of fields from a name: a call's result is computed anew at each read, and a `null` test of it proves nothing here (ADR-382 D4). Copy it into a local and test that: `let x = …`.
    = help: Say what an absent value counts as first: `(x ?? 0) + 1`, or take the value out once with `let v = x ?? return`.
